<section image-layout="left" id="zewh9bbjfy31f94"><img class="accent-image" id="kE_Vj1Bro7" type="image" alt="futuristic office with glowing screens and workers collaborating alongside AI interfaces, warm ambient light" src="https://cdn-staging.gamma.app/p7r4it3una5hm8z/generated-images/dt9d4sO11wi8JNe-EgLdA.png" source="image.ai-generated" meta="{&quot;width&quot;:768,&quot;height&quot;:1024}"><div class="body"><h1>AI &amp; the Labor Market: Is AI Disrupting the Future of Work?</h1><p>A clear-eyed look at what the evidence actually says — and what it means for workers, organizations, and policy.</p></div></section>
<section image-layout="blank" id="ygkne92l2nqq8ge"><div class="body"><columns col-widths="[35,65]"><div vertical-align="center"><img src="https://cdn-staging.gamma.app/acme/uploaded-images/VMSOvYY9Zfap-mZNFpbQI.png" source="image.custom" align="center"></div><div vertical-align="center"><h1>The Debate Is Real — and the Stakes Are Enormous</h1><smart-layout variant="stats" cell-size="15"><smart-layout-item label="$31T"><h4>U.S. Economy</h4><p>Nearly 60% is labor costs — even a small AI-driven shift moves trillions of dollars.</p></smart-layout-item><smart-layout-item label="300M"><h4>Jobs at Stake</h4><p>~27% of OECD positions could be transformed through AI automation globally.</p></smart-layout-item><smart-layout-item label="71%"><h4>Americans Worried</h4><p>Concerned AI will permanently put too many people out of work — but the reality is more nuanced.</p></smart-layout-item></smart-layout></div></columns></div></section>
<section image-layout="blank" id="a5xvtju18qhvcx1"><div class="body"><h1>A Job Is a Bundle of Tasks — Not a Single Thing</h1><columns col-widths="[65,35]"><div vertical-align="center"><img src="https://cdn-staging.gamma.app/acme/uploaded-images/MfIJj4MZa3u3zYdmzsCic.png" source="image.custom" align="center"></div><div vertical-align="center"><p>AI almost never wipes out whole jobs overnight. It affects <b>tasks first</b> — some get automated, some get supercharged, some create entirely new work.</p><aside variant="info"><p>A doctor diagnoses, orders tests, interprets results, and coordinates care. A lawyer drafts, researches, argues, and advises. <b>Each task has a different AI exposure profile.</b></p></aside><p>Understanding this task-level granularity is the key to cutting through the noise in the jobs debate.</p></div></columns></div></section>
<section image-layout="blank" id="gx1yxs2srsvbzlm"><div class="body"><h1>A Three-Layer Framework for Understanding AI's Impact</h1><img src="https://cdn-staging.gamma.app/acme/uploaded-images/tcJaZfVcfO2bxZF3Nbfe_.png" source="image.custom"><smart-layout variant="arrows" orientation="horizontal" cell-size="15"><smart-layout-item><h4>Layer 1 — Exposure</h4><p>Which tasks can AI plausibly assist or perform today?</p></smart-layout-item><smart-layout-item><h4>Layer 2 — Adoption</h4><p>How quickly will organizations actually deploy AI in practice?</p></smart-layout-item><smart-layout-item><h4>Layer 3 — Labor Market Response</h4><p>What is the net effect on employment, wages, and new job creation?</p></smart-layout-item></smart-layout><p>These layers constantly feed back into each other — which is why the debate is so contentious.</p></div></section>
<section image-layout="blank" id="sxkg3gi4f918m3i"><div class="body"><h1>Layer 1: Who Is Most Exposed?</h1><img src="https://cdn-staging.gamma.app/acme/uploaded-images/YUa98dwaWODFnA99ey7mK.png" source="image.custom" align="center"><smart-layout variant="outlineBoxesWithSideLine" cell-size="10"><smart-layout-item><h4>🔴 Highest Exposure</h4><p>Text-heavy, information-processing roles: legal research, financial analysis, programming, writing, customer support. High-paying, educated jobs score highest — their work is language and symbol dense.</p></smart-layout-item><smart-layout-item><h4>🟢 Lowest Exposure</h4><p>Embodied, on-site, and interpersonal roles: electricians, roofers, nursing assistants, athletes. Physical manipulation and rich social interaction remain largely AI-resistant today.</p></smart-layout-item></smart-layout></div></section>
<section image-layout="blank" id="lewtkucy0lguomz"><div class="body"><columns col-widths="[35,65]"><div vertical-align="center"><img src="https://cdn-staging.gamma.app/acme/uploaded-images/0EOfYbjRCdmBrLzAIiyt-.png" source="image.custom" align="center"></div><div vertical-align="center"><h1>AI Capabilities Are Racing Ahead of Expectations</h1></div></columns><columns col-widths="[50,50]"><div><h4>Benchmarks Crossed by 2024</h4><ul><li>Reading comprehension — surpassed human baseline</li><li>Visual reasoning — exceeded human performance</li><li>PhD-level science questions — at or above human level</li><li>Multimodal understanding — achieved in 2024</li></ul></div><div><h4>The "New Moore's Law"</h4><p>AI's autonomous task capacity doubles every <b>~7 months</b>.</p><ul><li><b>2026:</b> AI completes a 2-hour task</li><li><b>2027:</b> AI completes a full work day (8 hrs)</li><li><b>2028:</b> AI completes a full work week (40 hrs)</li><li><b>2029:</b> AI completes a full work month (167 hrs)</li></ul></div></columns></div></section>
<section image-layout="blank" id="vktf2vhfp7va7b6"><div class="body"><h1>Layer 2: Exposure ≠ Adoption</h1><columns col-widths="[35,65]"><div vertical-align="center"><p>A job can be highly exposed in theory but untouched in practice. Legal constraints, union rules, data confidentiality, and workflow inertia all slow real-world deployment.</p><aside variant="warning"><p>Even technically automatable tasks lag because of cultural and institutional friction — customer backlash, liability concerns, and lack of complementary investments.</p></aside></div><div vertical-align="center"><img src="https://cdn-staging.gamma.app/acme/uploaded-images/36dqHYHpPqMq2xGj1uh38.png" source="image.custom" align="center"><p>Generative AI hit <b>54.6%</b> overall adult adoption by August 2025 — up 10 points in a single year. Work-specific adoption reached <b>37.4%</b>, still trailing personal use.</p></div></columns></div></section>
<section image-layout="blank" id="5g1mt4saen6le2i"><div class="body"><h1>The Productivity J-Curve: Why Gains Take Time</h1><columns col-widths="[35,65]"><div vertical-align="center"><img src="https://cdn-staging.gamma.app/acme/uploaded-images/q-WW-hRgYtpndVD52_C_A.png" source="image.custom" align="center"></div><div vertical-align="center"><smart-layout variant="solidBoxes" cell-size="20"><smart-layout-item><h4>Historical Precedent</h4><p>Electricity took decades to raise factory output. PC productivity gains only showed up in U.S. statistics in the mid-1990s — <b>15 years after launch</b>. AI is a general-purpose technology on the same trajectory.</p></smart-layout-item><smart-layout-item><h4>The Dip Before the Rise</h4><p>Early adoption often <b>dips productivity first</b>. 40% of knowledge workers report spending ~2 hours/week fixing low-quality AI outputs — before gains accelerate as workflows mature.</p></smart-layout-item></smart-layout></div></columns></div></section>
<section image-layout="blank" id="oxqk47n8oy7h51m"><div class="body"><h1>Response 1: Augmentation — AI Makes Workers More Productive</h1><smart-layout variant="imagesText" shape-size="md" image-position="top" image-shape="landscape" cell-size="10"><smart-layout-item><img class="accessory-image" src="https://cdn-staging.gamma.app/p7r4it3una5hm8z/generated-images/9VH0O4gS9f4p7ydVAl3af.png" source="image.ai-generated" alt="knowledge worker at a clean desk using AI software on a laptop, bright modern office, natural daylight"><div><h4>Daily AI Users Win</h4><p><b>41%</b> of daily AI users rate their productivity "Very Good" vs. <b>26%</b> of non-users. Gains extend to focus, stress management, and job satisfaction.</p></div></smart-layout-item><smart-layout-item><img class="accessory-image" src="https://cdn-staging.gamma.app/p7r4it3una5hm8z/generated-images/NhucSLiy7BNvXbg1_H9NO.png" source="image.ai-generated" alt="radiologist reviewing medical scans on large screens in a darkened hospital reading room"><div><h4>The Radiology Lesson</h4><p>Geoff Hinton predicted radiologists replaced within 5 years (2016). He was right that AI outperforms on benchmarks — but wrong about demand. Radiology <b>continues to grow</b>, a pattern seen across many exposed professions.</p></div></smart-layout-item></smart-layout></div></section>
<section image-layout="blank" id="96nzf3a0hyodrde"><div class="body"><h1>Response 2: Displacement — Real, But Concentrated</h1><columns col-widths="[65,35]"><div vertical-align="center"><img src="https://cdn-staging.gamma.app/acme/uploaded-images/bbGVZndFwjHmGLZpma0Bu.png" source="image.custom" align="center"></div><div vertical-align="center"><p>Clerical and back-office roles face the highest displacement risk: routine document processing, booking, transcription, and standardized communications are repeatedly flagged as highly automatable.</p><blockquote><p>"Look for jobs that are repetitive, independent, closed, short, forgiving, and digital — those are the ones most at risk on shorter timescales."</p><p><i>— Andrej Karpathy</i></p></blockquote></div></columns></div></section>
<section image-layout="left" id="v8ka2xld8sxoalc"><img class="accent-image" id="hLO1BAMSpB" type="image" alt="diverse team of professionals collaborating in a modern co-working space, new technology tools visible, energetic atmosphere" src="https://cdn-staging.gamma.app/p7r4it3una5hm8z/generated-images/Uzq6gVaujJqqKQg9zq1xn.png" source="image.ai-generated" meta="{&quot;width&quot;:768,&quot;height&quot;:1024}"><div class="body"><h1>Response 3: Reinstatement — AI Creates New Roles Too</h1><smart-layout variant="iconsText" image-position="left" cell-size="xl"><smart-layout-item><icon class="accessory-image" src="https://cdn-staging.gamma.app/_app_static/icons/v0/rectangle-history.svg" source="image.icon" alt="history clock"></icon><div><h4>History's Pattern</h4><p>Technological revolutions rarely destroy jobs in aggregate — they transform them. The PC era created software engineers, IT support, and digital marketers that didn't exist before.</p></div></smart-layout-item><smart-layout-item><icon class="accessory-image" src="https://cdn-staging.gamma.app/_app_static/icons/v0/robot.svg" source="image.icon" alt="robot engineer"></icon><div><h4>New AI Occupations</h4><p>AI is already spawning AI engineers, prompt specialists, AI trainers, and trust &amp; safety roles. Productivity gains lower costs, raise demand, and expand existing occupations.</p></div></smart-layout-item><smart-layout-item><icon class="accessory-image" src="https://cdn-staging.gamma.app/_app_static/icons/v0/scale-balanced.svg" source="image.icon" alt="balance scale fairness"></icon><div><h4>The Real Challenge</h4><p>Pace and <b>distribution</b> of change — who adapts fastest and who benefits most — will define AI's true labor market legacy.</p></div></smart-layout-item></smart-layout></div></section>
<section image-layout="blank" id="lm7cn9sym50tzdn"><div class="body"><h1>What to Watch: Three Questions That Will Determine the Outcome</h1><img src="https://cdn-staging.gamma.app/acme/uploaded-images/9yfLWL3zkz5f1WStGFzWZ.png" source="image.custom" align="center"><smart-layout variant="outlineBoxesWithLabel" cell-size="10" numbered="true"><smart-layout-item><h4>Will adoption accelerate or plateau?</h4><p>If AI capability doubles every 4–7 months, the window for workers and institutions to adapt narrows dramatically.</p></smart-layout-item><smart-layout-item><h4>Who captures the productivity gains?</h4><p>Higher-wage, higher-educated workers are most exposed — and most positioned to benefit. Lower-wage workers face less disruption but fewer gains.</p></smart-layout-item><smart-layout-item><h4>How will policy respond?</h4><p>Retraining, labor protections, and safety nets will shape whether AI's abundance is broadly shared or concentrated at the top.</p></smart-layout-item></smart-layout></div></section>
<section image-layout="blank" id="ihs8cfcbq0nfpdw"><div class="body"><h1>Sources</h1><ul><li><a href="#">AI and labor market PDF</a></li></ul></div></section>